  "version": 1,
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Inverse Proportion: A Fixed Total Gets Shared",
    "description": "Recognize fixed-work situations where more of one quantity means less of another.",
    "teachingApproach": "Use a fixed cake of work as the mental model: more workers means each needs less time, so worker × time remains constant. Extend the same invariant idea to speed and time, gears and revolutions, and rope turns around a cylinder. Include reverse questions with missing workers and absentees. Every block asks what stays fixed before applying the inverse rule.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 15, Chain Rule. Approved scope: Teach inverse proportion through a fixed total: for the same work, workers × time stays constant; for the same distance, speed × time stays constant; for meshed gears, teeth × revolutions stays constant; for a fixed rope around cylinders, circumference × turns stays constant. Include missing-workforce problems and reverse reasoning. Make the learner ask what is being held fixed before deciding 'more means less', and guard against treating inverse situations as direct ones."
  },
  "scriptId": "cr-02-inverse-proportion-fixed-work",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "schemaVersion": 1
